  "note": "A1+ Unit 1 Lesson 1.5 rebuilt on 2026-11-11 under the A1+ lesson standards approved 2026-10-27 and the user's 2026-11-09 rule that every A1+ lesson follows up the A1 lesson with the same number. A1 1.5 Brothers, Sisters and Adults names brothers and sisters (He is a brother. They are sisters.) and adds an (an adult). A1+ 1.5 asks how many brothers and sisters someone has and says who is older or younger. Family map: Ana has two sisters, an older one and a younger one; Luis has two brothers, an older one and a younger one; Diego has one brother. The siblings have no names and never speak. Luis asks Ana, and Ana asks Luis and Diego: Ana and Sofia share one voice in the course cast, as do Luis and Diego, so the asker never sounds like the person answering (user decision 2026-11-11 at the listening gate). Older siblings look about 48 and younger ones about 20, so they read as brothers and sisters of a cast member in their thirties, not as parents or children. A sentence with has is true of its person whatever photo shows them, so a sentence about Ana never competes with another photo of Ana; the older/younger contrast stays with This is my ..., where this is the sibling shown. The previous 1.5 (What I usually do: always, usually, sometimes, never, every day) returns as 1.6, which follows A1 1.6 with the frequency words.",
